{#
  The pod-spec "shell" every long-running workload here shares, at its two depths.

  Until 2026-09-19 each of the 69 Deployment and DaemonSet templates hand-copied these fields
  and three field-level guards policed the copies (#2056). A copy is where a field drifts: the
  next service inherits whatever its author copied, and nothing notices until a pod is evicted
  ahead of the things it outranks on paper, or an app reads a Service's env vars as its own
  configuration. Two macros, because the fields sit at two indent depths and a macro that can
  emit at two depths is one that can emit at the wrong one (security-context.yml.j2 says the
  same). Both emit at one fixed depth: `spec_shell` at 2 spaces, directly under a Deployment's
  `spec:`; `pod_shell` at 6 spaces, directly under `spec.template.spec:`. A Job or CronJob pod
  spec sits elsewhere and is NOT a caller.

  ansible/tests/k8s/test_workload_shell_uses_the_macros.py is the call-site guard: every
  Deployment template calls both, every DaemonSet template calls `pod_shell`, and none writes
  an owned field out by hand. A macro cannot force its own call, so the rendered-fleet census
  in test_pod_template_hygiene.py, test_deploy_strategy.py and
  test_deployment_revision_history_limit.py stays — it is the half that tells an omission from
  a macro call, which a grep cannot.

  ── spec_shell(strategy, max_surge=none, max_unavailable=none) ── Deployments only ──

    revisionHistoryLimit: 3   invariant. Unpinned, a Deployment keeps ten scaled-to-zero
                              ReplicaSets and `kubectl get rs -A` stops being readable — 605
                              ReplicaSets against 68 Deployments on 2026-08-28. Rollbacks here
                              are `git revert` + redeploy, so deeper history buys nothing.
    strategy                  REQUIRED, `Recreate` or `RollingUpdate`, no default. Recreate is
                              a downtime gap on every deploy; RollingUpdate is two pods at once
                              against whatever the volume and the app allow. The choice is the
                              decision test_deploy_strategy.py records — a Recreate must sit in
                              its `_RECREATE` allowlist with the reason — so it is made at the
                              call, never inherited.
    max_surge, max_unavailable
                              the `rollingUpdate` tunables, RollingUpdate only; omitted when
                              none, which leaves the API defaults (25% / 25%).

  A DaemonSet has no ReplicaSets and no `strategy` (its `updateStrategy` is a different field
  with different values), so it does not call this.

  ── pod_shell(priority_class, service_account=none, automount=none, run_as_user=none,
               run_as_group=none, fs_group=none, non_root=false) ──

    enableServiceLinks: false invariant, every caller. Kubernetes otherwise injects legacy
                              Docker-link env vars for every Service in the namespace —
                              AUTHELIA_SERVICE_HOST, AUTHELIA_PORT_9091_TCP and friends — and an
                              app that reads <NAME>_* as configuration picks them up. Authelia
                              did, and exited before serving anything (daniel-box, 2026-08-02):
                                the new key already exists with value 'tcp4://:9091' but the
                                deprecated keys and the new key can't both be configured
                              The collision needs only that a Service name match the app's
                              env-var prefix, which is the normal case here.
    priority_class            REQUIRED, one of the homelab tiers in
                              roles/setup/k3s/templates/priorityclass.yaml.j2 (or a k8s
                              system class the hygiene test's `_SYSTEM_TIER` allowlists). No
                              default on purpose: five values are in use and none dominates,
                              and an omitted class is priority 0 — BELOW homelab-best-effort,
                              so the pod loses every eviction race silently (#1851).
    service_account           emit `serviceAccountName: <value>`; omitted when none.
    automount                 `automountServiceAccountToken`. Left at none it follows the
                              hygiene rule: a pod that names no service_account runs as the
                              namespace `default` SA and never uses its token, so `false` is
                              emitted (#1852); a pod that names one gets NO line, because the
                              ServiceAccount object may set the field and the pod's own value
                              then means nothing. Pass true or false to write the line
                              regardless — headlamp needs true beside its SA, since
                              `-in-cluster` reads the API address and CA off the projected mount.
    run_as_user, run_as_group, fs_group, non_root
                              the pod-level `securityContext`, emitted only when at least one is
                              given; omitted keys stay OMITTED, never defaulted, because an
                              absent `runAsNonRoot` and an explicit false are different
                              admission outcomes. This block is all-or-nothing: a pod whose
                              securityContext carries anything else (sysctls, supplementalGroups)
                              writes the whole block itself at the call site and passes none of
                              these four — two `securityContext:` keys under one pod spec is a
                              silent last-wins in YAML, and the rendered census would read the
                              survivor as the intent.

  What stays at the call site, beside the call: `nodeSelector` (a pin with a per-site reason
  and its own guard, test_vip_pins.py, which reads the literal), `affinity`
  (pod-affinity.yml.j2), `hostNetwork`/`hostPID`, `tolerations`, `hostAliases`,
  `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, `progressDeadlineSeconds` — one caller each, so there is
  nothing to share. The WHY comment for a non-default argument stays above the call, where
  the copied blocks used to sit.
#}
{% macro spec_shell(strategy, max_surge=none, max_unavailable=none) %}
  revisionHistoryLimit: 3
  strategy:
    type: {{ strategy }}
{% if max_surge is not none or max_unavailable is not none %}
    rollingUpdate:
{% if max_surge is not none %}
      maxSurge: {{ max_surge }}
{% endif %}
{% if max_unavailable is not none %}
      maxUnavailable: {{ max_unavailable }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro pod_shell(priority_class, service_account=none, automount=none, run_as_user=none, run_as_group=none, fs_group=none, non_root=false) %}
      enableServiceLinks: false
      priorityClassName: {{ priority_class }}
{% if service_account is not none %}
      serviceAccountName: {{ service_account }}
{% endif %}
{% if automount is not none %}
      automountServiceAccountToken: {{ 'true' if automount else 'false' }}
{% elif service_account is none %}
      automountServiceAccountToken: false
{% endif %}
{% if run_as_user is not none or run_as_group is not none or fs_group is not none or non_root %}
      securityContext:
{% if non_root %}
        runAsNonRoot: true
{% endif %}
{% if run_as_user is not none %}
        runAsUser: {{ run_as_user }}
{% endif %}
{% if run_as_group is not none %}
        runAsGroup: {{ run_as_group }}
{% endif %}
{% if fs_group is not none %}
        fsGroup: {{ fs_group }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
